1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,500
During World War II, when the Allies invaded Iran,

2
00:00:04,540 --> 00:00:08,360
Dear Uncle's fantasies took on a real shape,

3
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:12,580
and his fear of English revenge bordered on madness.

4
00:00:13,280 --> 00:00:17,640
The only cure was to end the English feud with him.

5
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:23,860
That required an English envoy to take part in peace talks.

6
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:31,180
Fate had decreed that Dear Uncle would share the fate of Napoleon Bonaparte.

7
00:03:13,880 --> 00:03:15,030
Hello.

8
00:03:15,080 --> 00:03:17,670
Uncle Asdollah, Farrokh Lagha.

9
00:03:19,640 --> 00:03:21,990
That owl's turned up around here again.

10
00:03:22,040 --> 00:03:24,300
Well, well, what a gathering!

11
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:26,160
What brings you here?

12
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:29,280
I went to pay my condolences at Monir Khanom's house.

13
00:03:29,320 --> 00:03:31,780
On my way back I thought I'd drop in on you.

14
00:03:31,820 --> 00:03:33,260
Which Monir Khanom?

15
00:03:33,300 --> 00:03:36,620
Monir Khanom, Etemad-ol-Mamalek's daughter!

16
00:03:36,660 --> 00:03:37,500
Oh, yes.

17
00:03:37,540 --> 00:03:39,960
Poor thing, she's had such bad luck lately.

18
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:42,240
Her poor husband came home from the office at noon,

19
00:03:42,280 --> 00:03:45,160
went to the pool to wash his face, and had a stroke.

20
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:46,060
Fell into the water.

21
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:49,840
Poor man wasn't even old. He was gone before the doctor came.

22
00:03:49,880 --> 00:03:52,280
The memorial service is this evening.

23
00:03:52,320 --> 00:03:55,640
Everyone says grief over his brother-in-law killed him.

24
00:03:56,940 --> 00:03:59,020
What happened to his brother-in-law?

25
00:03:59,060 --> 00:04:03,720
The English arrested him and some others the other day and exiled them.

26
00:04:03,760 --> 00:04:07,325
They say they sent them all to Arak in chains.

27
00:04:07,940 --> 00:04:09,980
The English? Why?

28
00:04:10,020 --> 00:04:13,800
Bless me, this family is so big

29
00:04:13,840 --> 00:04:16,120
you have to go to somebody's funeral every day!

30
00:04:16,160 --> 00:04:18,360
Asdollah, wait a moment.

31
00:04:19,640 --> 00:04:20,440
Madam,

32
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:22,880
you said the English

33
00:04:22,920 --> 00:04:25,440
arrested a group of people and sent them to Arak?

34
00:04:25,480 --> 00:04:26,960
Yes, Sir.

35
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:29,260
Poor Etemad's brother-in-law

36
00:04:29,300 --> 00:04:30,200
was one of them.

37
00:04:30,240 --> 00:04:33,500
Poor man had nothing to do with any of that.

38
00:04:33,540 --> 00:04:35,080
The English...

39
00:04:36,040 --> 00:04:38,500
The English have made their move.

40
00:04:39,320 --> 00:04:40,480
Pouri!

41
00:04:40,960 --> 00:04:41,690
Ghasem!

42
00:04:41,740 --> 00:04:43,225
Let's go home.

43
00:04:43,820 --> 00:04:44,980
Let's go.

44
00:04:53,660 --> 00:04:55,840
Why did Sir get so upset?

45
00:04:55,880 --> 00:05:00,320
Sir barely knew Monir Khanom's husband or his brother-in-law.

46
00:05:01,840 --> 00:05:04,540
Sir is upset about poor Mansur-ol-Saltaneh.

47
00:05:04,580 --> 00:05:06,080
Dustali's uncle.

48
00:05:06,120 --> 00:05:09,780
Why, what's wrong with Dustali's uncle?

49
00:05:09,820 --> 00:05:10,620
Strange.

50
00:05:10,660 --> 00:05:13,320
Strange that you haven't heard, madam.

51
00:05:13,360 --> 00:05:14,650
Poor man.

52
00:05:15,600 --> 00:05:16,840
God rest his soul.

53
00:05:16,880 --> 00:05:18,400
He suffered so much in his illness.

54
00:05:18,440 --> 00:05:21,485
Good heavens! How did I not hear?

55
00:05:22,140 --> 00:05:24,620
Your late mother was so close to that family,

56
00:05:24,660 --> 00:05:27,220
I thought you'd be the one to close his eyes.

57
00:05:27,260 --> 00:05:29,140
You're right. How awful.

58
00:05:29,180 --> 00:05:32,420
I'd better go see Lady Aziz while it's early.

59
00:05:33,300 --> 00:05:34,940
Neat trick you pulled on her!

60
00:05:34,980 --> 00:05:36,880
The owl's gone back to her ruins.

61
00:05:36,920 --> 00:05:39,560
First that idiot Dustali wrecked our plan,

62
00:05:39,600 --> 00:05:41,100
and now this woman.

63
00:05:41,140 --> 00:05:42,760
She really is just like an owl.

64
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:45,600
It wasn't all Dustali's fault.

65
00:05:45,640 --> 00:05:47,720
Believe me, deep down, Sir,

66
00:05:47,760 --> 00:05:49,900
without even realizing it himself,

67
00:05:49,940 --> 00:05:51,950
longs to be taken prisoner.

68
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:53,500
I have no doubt he's waiting

69
00:05:53,540 --> 00:05:56,585
for a fate like Napoleon's.

70
00:05:56,860 --> 00:05:58,080
Even if the Indian corporal

71
00:05:58,120 --> 00:05:59,980
had granted him every concession,

72
00:06:00,020 --> 00:06:02,260
he'd still have found some excuse in the end

73
00:06:02,300 --> 00:06:04,140
and set Shir-Ali the butcher on him

74
00:06:04,180 --> 00:06:06,510
to wreck the talks.

75
00:06:06,604 --> 00:06:10,169
Honestly, I'm afraid Sir will do himself harm.

76
00:06:10,584 --> 00:06:14,140
Remember him talking about Napoleon taking poison the other day?

77
00:06:14,180 --> 00:06:16,640
After his defeat by the Allied armies.

78
00:06:16,680 --> 00:06:19,595
That's not what worries me.

79
00:06:19,924 --> 00:06:23,640
The first time Napoleon had to abdicate, he took poison.

80
00:06:23,680 --> 00:06:26,240
But the second time, he just waited

81
00:06:26,280 --> 00:06:29,325
until they came and exiled him to St Helena.

82
00:06:30,564 --> 00:06:32,560
I feel so sorry for the old man.

83
00:06:32,600 --> 00:06:34,560
I'm at my wits' end.

84
00:06:34,600 --> 00:06:36,995
We'll have to see what happens.

85
00:06:44,524 --> 00:06:45,560
Asdollah, I have an idea.

86
00:06:45,600 --> 00:06:48,560
What if I had a word with Sardar Maharat-Khan myself?

87
00:06:48,600 --> 00:06:49,560
About Sir?

88
00:06:49,600 --> 00:06:50,560
No.

89
00:06:50,600 --> 00:06:55,560
About the rug that charlatan, Corporal Eshtiaq Khan, pocketed!

90
00:06:55,600 --> 00:07:00,600
Had I made some vow to give that corporal an Isfahan rug?

91
00:07:01,324 --> 00:07:03,320
On top of it all, my face ended up like this,

92
00:07:03,360 --> 00:07:05,820
and I lost my rug too!

93
00:07:06,444 --> 00:07:08,360
Moment, moment, Colonel!

94
00:07:08,400 --> 00:07:09,920
Are you going to make a fuss again?

95
00:07:09,960 --> 00:07:12,380
Good Lord, Colonel!

96
00:07:12,420 --> 00:07:14,140
Your brother is wasting away

97
00:07:14,180 --> 00:07:15,560
and you're thinking of your rug?

98
00:07:15,600 --> 00:07:17,140
Didn't you say you were ready

99
00:07:17,180 --> 00:07:19,500
to give your life for Brother?

100
00:07:19,540 --> 00:07:20,635
Yes.

101
00:07:21,304 --> 00:07:23,220
I'm not worried about Brother.

102
00:07:23,260 --> 00:07:26,240
Brother is no reed to shake in every wind.

103
00:07:26,280 --> 00:07:29,620
A man who's spent his whole life in war and battle

104
00:07:29,660 --> 00:07:33,500
surely knows how to bear life's ups and downs.

105
00:07:33,540 --> 00:07:35,285
Besides,

106
00:07:35,764 --> 00:07:38,520
Shazdeh, that charlatan did nothing for us.

107
00:07:38,560 --> 00:07:40,420
All right, don't get worked up now.

108
00:07:40,460 --> 00:07:42,200
Go and get your rug back from him.

109
00:07:42,240 --> 00:07:43,040
Fair point.

110
00:07:43,080 --> 00:07:45,540
I don't know where to find that charlatan.

111
00:07:45,580 --> 00:07:51,620
Well, you see, Corporal Eshtiaq Khan was to leave on a mission today.

112
00:07:51,660 --> 00:07:53,760
Leave? For where?

113
00:07:53,800 --> 00:07:56,974
He gave me the address. Write it down, write it down.

114
00:07:57,024 --> 00:07:58,154
Write.

115
00:07:58,204 --> 00:08:00,480
Write him a letter first thing tomorrow,

116
00:08:00,520 --> 00:08:05,220
to the address I give you, and he'll send your rugs right back. Go on.

117
00:08:06,664 --> 00:08:08,149
Tell me the address.

118
00:08:08,424 --> 00:08:15,200
Corporal Eshtiaq Khan, Ta... Tank Division Thirty-Eight,

119
00:08:16,784 --> 00:08:20,360
El Alamein front, North Africa.

120
00:08:20,400 --> 00:08:22,400
Got that?

121
00:08:22,920 --> 00:08:26,460
To hell with your ugly mug, Shazdeh!

122
00:08:26,500 --> 00:08:27,920
Yes, Sir?

123
00:08:28,080 --> 00:08:29,695
What's wrong, Sir?

124
00:08:36,004 --> 00:08:40,340
That afternoon Mash Ghasem brought word that Leili wanted to see me.

125
00:08:41,364 --> 00:08:45,080
Dear Uncle had summoned her and Pouri to his bedside

126
00:08:45,120 --> 00:08:51,380
and told them the English would soon exile him somewhere he'd never return from.

127
00:08:52,004 --> 00:08:58,540
He told them to be ready to marry the moment the English came.

128
00:08:59,604 --> 00:09:00,740
I asked Leili,

129
00:09:00,780 --> 00:09:03,240
What did you tell them, Leili?

130
00:09:03,280 --> 00:09:06,260
What could I say? Daddy's ill.

131
00:09:06,904 --> 00:09:10,720
If I'd said I won't marry Pouri, he'd have gotten worse.

132
00:09:10,760 --> 00:09:13,480
Should've told Dear Uncle you've long loved me, not Pouri.

133
00:09:13,520 --> 00:09:15,740
Said you won't marry that Feshfeshoo!

134
00:09:15,780 --> 00:09:17,180
Why didn't you tell him?

135
00:09:17,220 --> 00:09:18,840
If Daddy were well,

136
00:09:18,880 --> 00:09:21,260
if he weren't ill, I'd have told him.

137
00:09:22,324 --> 00:09:24,524
Saeid, I'm so scared.

138
00:09:25,044 --> 00:09:29,500
I'm scared if I talk back, God forbid, he'll die on the spot.

139
00:09:29,540 --> 00:09:32,325
Daddy is very ill, Saeid.

140
00:09:32,844 --> 00:09:35,240
I don't know what to do, I just don't know.

141
00:09:35,280 --> 00:09:38,620
Maybe when Daddy gets better, he'll change his mind.

142
00:09:38,660 --> 00:09:42,780
If you want to marry Pouri or anyone else, go ahead. I won't live.

143
00:09:43,604 --> 00:09:47,760
If you love me, if my life matters, tell your dad you'll wait for me.

144
00:10:22,140 --> 00:10:24,140
Let me see.

145
00:10:24,183 --> 00:10:26,383
Look at me.

146
00:10:26,884 --> 00:10:28,889
Are you crying?

147
00:10:29,064 --> 00:10:31,069
A great big lout like you,

148
00:10:32,064 --> 00:10:35,044
crying like a little girl?

149
00:10:37,004 --> 00:10:38,100
Listen.

150
00:10:38,140 --> 00:10:40,100
I'm serious.

151
00:10:40,140 --> 00:10:42,535
Listen carefully to what I say.

152
00:10:42,924 --> 00:10:44,974
I'm not joking at all.

153
00:10:45,024 --> 00:10:48,174
Since you haven't got the guts to go to San Francisco,

154
00:10:48,224 --> 00:10:52,049
at least act like you've been to San Francisco.

155
00:10:52,124 --> 00:10:54,259
Moment! Don't interrupt me.

156
00:10:55,744 --> 00:10:58,034
Go talk to Leili, win her over.

157
00:10:58,084 --> 00:11:01,854
Act as if you two are just back from San Francisco.

158
00:11:01,904 --> 00:11:05,794
Instead of crying, put on the look of a weary traveler.

159
00:11:05,844 --> 00:11:10,164
Then Dear Uncle will have to wait a few years till you're a bigger lout,

160
00:11:10,544 --> 00:11:16,184
or strike up the wedding music right now, believe me.

161
00:11:18,024 --> 00:11:22,820
She won't agree, Uncle Asdollah. Even if I did, she wouldn't. I know it.

162
00:11:22,860 --> 00:11:25,100
Fine, then let her marry that genius Pouri,

163
00:11:25,140 --> 00:11:28,200
that wheezing Arab stallion!

164
00:11:28,240 --> 00:11:30,600
Uncle Asdollah!

165
00:11:31,604 --> 00:11:33,480
Can't you think of any other way?

166
00:11:33,520 --> 00:11:35,100
Sure!

167
00:11:35,140 --> 00:11:36,300
Sure!

168
00:11:36,800 --> 00:11:39,934
The other way is for you to marry me!

169
00:11:39,984 --> 00:11:41,599
Uncle Asdollah!

170
00:11:46,884 --> 00:11:50,384
What Farrokh Lagha said yesterday is true.

171
00:11:50,784 --> 00:11:53,800
The Allies have arrested a number of pro-German statesmen

172
00:11:53,840 --> 00:11:56,554
and sent them to Arak.

173
00:11:56,604 --> 00:11:58,120
Once that news is official,

174
00:11:58,160 --> 00:11:59,360
the first thing Dear Uncle will do

175
00:11:59,400 --> 00:12:03,034
is pack Leili's suitcase and send her off to her husband.

176
00:12:03,084 --> 00:12:04,240
Get a move on.

177
00:12:04,280 --> 00:12:06,620
Couldn't you talk to Dear Uncle?

178
00:12:06,660 --> 00:12:08,200
Tell him everything.

179
00:12:08,240 --> 00:12:11,860
Moment, moment, moment, my boy!

180
00:12:11,900 --> 00:12:14,400
Rest assured, if Dear Uncle hears of any of this,

181
00:12:14,440 --> 00:12:17,400
he'll drag Seyed Abolghasem the preacher down from the pulpit

182
00:12:17,440 --> 00:12:20,745
to marry Leili to Pouri on the spot.

183
00:12:21,400 --> 00:12:22,860
Then what should I do, Uncle Asdollah?

184
00:12:22,900 --> 00:12:23,860
Travel.

185
00:12:23,900 --> 00:12:25,620
Travel is a very fine thing.

186
00:12:25,660 --> 00:12:28,510
I'm going on a trip myself one of these days.

187
00:12:28,900 --> 00:12:30,860
Only I'm an unlucky man.

188
00:12:30,900 --> 00:12:34,010
Instead of San Francisco, they're sending me to Beirut.

189
00:12:34,060 --> 00:12:35,675
Uncle Asdollah!

190
00:13:11,900 --> 00:13:14,945
Thank God you're better, my boy.

191
00:13:16,360 --> 00:13:18,900
We were very worried last night.

192
00:13:19,640 --> 00:13:21,620
This morning we came to see how you were.

193
00:13:21,660 --> 00:13:23,750
The doctor was at your bedside.

194
00:13:23,800 --> 00:13:26,845
You were raving all night, my boy.

195
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:31,500
What time is it? What's the time, Mash Ghasem?

196
00:13:31,700 --> 00:13:33,445
I'm late for school.

197
00:13:34,040 --> 00:13:35,850
It's noon, my boy.

198
00:13:35,940 --> 00:13:37,920
Too bad, I missed school.

199
00:13:37,960 --> 00:13:41,720
We wish the doctor could see you now.

200
00:13:41,900 --> 00:13:45,400
The heathen said last night you'd surely got typhoid.

201
00:13:46,440 --> 00:13:51,120
Good Lord, these doctors can't tell a cow from a cello!

202
00:13:52,300 --> 00:13:53,720
I feel weak.

203
00:13:56,320 --> 00:13:58,430
Why are you all dressed up?

204
00:13:58,480 --> 00:13:59,600
Huh?

205
00:14:03,220 --> 00:14:05,225
Are you going somewhere?

206
00:14:07,760 --> 00:14:10,220
Well, my boy, why should I lie?

207
00:14:10,580 --> 00:14:12,195
The grave is this close!

208
00:14:13,900 --> 00:14:16,900
We'll have to go any day now.

209
00:14:17,900 --> 00:14:22,900
This may be the last time you ever see us.

210
00:14:24,900 --> 00:14:26,900
Where are you going?

211
00:14:27,900 --> 00:14:31,270
The English may be on their way right now.

212
00:14:31,900 --> 00:14:35,850
Forgive us for anything we ever did to you, my boy.

213
00:14:35,900 --> 00:14:37,490
How is Dear Uncle?

214
00:14:37,540 --> 00:14:38,700
Oh dear, oh dear, oh dear!

215
00:14:38,740 --> 00:14:42,960
Don't ask... God spare every Muslim such a thing.

216
00:14:43,580 --> 00:14:46,790
Since he saw those treacherous English last night, Sir

217
00:14:46,840 --> 00:14:49,235
looks twenty years older.

218
00:14:50,220 --> 00:14:52,615
He didn't sleep a wink all night.

219
00:14:52,780 --> 00:14:56,605
The poor soul seemed to be writing his will.

220
00:14:56,740 --> 00:14:58,875
How is he today?

221
00:14:59,260 --> 00:15:01,720
Thank God, he's a bit better today.

222
00:15:02,420 --> 00:15:04,425
He got all his fretting done last night.

223
00:15:05,240 --> 00:15:07,180
Has Leili gone to school?

224
00:15:09,920 --> 00:15:12,240
Where have you been, my boy?

225
00:15:13,220 --> 00:15:18,420
At dawn today Sir sent everyone off with the Colonel to Ab-Ali.

226
00:15:19,040 --> 00:15:19,880
Where?

227
00:15:19,920 --> 00:15:22,260
Ab-Ali, the Colonel's country garden.

228
00:15:22,800 --> 00:15:23,960
Why?

229
00:15:25,220 --> 00:15:27,420
To keep them far from Tehran.

230
00:15:30,420 --> 00:15:31,905
My boy,

231
00:15:33,220 --> 00:15:35,225
well, Sir is right.

232
00:15:39,740 --> 00:15:44,920
The kids shouldn't see the English drag Sir and us off in chains.

233
00:15:46,240 --> 00:15:48,130
I mean, who knows?

234
00:15:48,180 --> 00:15:51,160
They might do the kids harm too.

235
00:15:52,040 --> 00:15:55,605
Those treacherous English are capable of anything.

236
00:15:55,760 --> 00:15:58,300
Any news? Hasn't Wellington come to take Sir yet?

237
00:15:58,340 --> 00:16:01,380
Because of Saeid, I haven't been to see him today.

238
00:16:01,420 --> 00:16:03,180
But I just asked Mash Ghasem.

239
00:16:03,220 --> 00:16:06,240
He said he's calmed down a bit since sending the kids away.

240
00:16:06,280 --> 00:16:07,900
He said he slept in his clothes last night.

241
00:16:07,940 --> 00:16:09,780
Let's go and see how he is.

242
00:16:09,820 --> 00:16:11,180
How's Saeid?

243
00:16:11,220 --> 00:16:12,180
Mash Ghasem is with him.

244
00:16:12,220 --> 00:16:15,525
You look in on him while I get dressed.

245
00:16:18,920 --> 00:16:22,360
Last night Sir ordered the kids and Miss Leili

246
00:16:22,400 --> 00:16:24,360
to go with the Colonel to Ab-Ali.

247
00:16:24,400 --> 00:16:27,640
Didn't he say how long the kids must stay there?

248
00:16:28,160 --> 00:16:30,360
Well, my boy, why should I lie?

249
00:16:30,400 --> 00:16:35,360
Maybe till the English come and carry Sir and us off as prisoners.

250
00:16:35,400 --> 00:16:38,400
What if the English don't come?

251
00:16:40,400 --> 00:16:44,400
You're still just a child, my boy.

252
00:16:45,400 --> 00:16:48,400
What do you know about the English?

253
00:16:50,200 --> 00:16:54,200
Sir and we haven't had our clothes off since yesterday.

254
00:16:54,980 --> 00:16:59,400
We've packed two pots of food so we don't starve on the road to Arak.

255
00:17:01,960 --> 00:17:06,740
The English feed prisoners brick-rubble soup with Indian snake oil.

256
00:17:09,880 --> 00:17:12,380
We had a fellow townsman once who...

257
00:17:12,420 --> 00:17:13,980
Mash Ghasem, hurry, Sir wants you.

258
00:17:14,020 --> 00:17:14,940
Hello, Uncle Asdollah.

259
00:17:14,980 --> 00:17:18,155
Oh, good Lord! Looks like the English are here!

260
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:23,940
Hello, Uncle Asdollah.

261
00:17:25,560 --> 00:17:30,520
Funny, that dimwit Naser-ol-Hokama was sure you had typhoid.

262
00:17:32,120 --> 00:17:35,600
Thank heaven he didn't say the boy had VD!

263
00:17:35,640 --> 00:17:36,900
Nothing to be ashamed of.

264
00:17:36,940 --> 00:17:39,900
Haven't you come up with a way yet? Any idea?

265
00:17:39,940 --> 00:17:41,100
Sure!

266
00:17:42,100 --> 00:17:46,420
Last night at your Dear Uncle's, I brought you up on some pretext.

267
00:17:46,460 --> 00:17:47,880
What did Dear Uncle say?

268
00:17:47,920 --> 00:17:49,050
Moment.

269
00:17:49,100 --> 00:17:50,600
Moment.

270
00:17:51,440 --> 00:17:52,730
Listen.

271
00:17:54,760 --> 00:17:58,760
Actually, it's good you know what he said. It'll set your mind at rest.

272
00:17:59,740 --> 00:18:01,970
When your name came up, all he said was,

273
00:18:02,020 --> 00:18:06,040
"A wolf cub grows into a wolf, though raised among men."

274
00:18:08,360 --> 00:18:10,885
What did you say, Uncle Asdollah?

275
00:18:11,700 --> 00:18:13,705
Please, tell me.

276
00:18:15,580 --> 00:18:17,680
Please.

277
00:18:20,500 --> 00:18:24,480
What did you expect me to say after that?

278
00:18:24,520 --> 00:18:28,345
"I've come to ask your daughter's hand for the wolf cub"?

279
00:18:35,150 --> 00:18:39,140
Honestly, I could think of nothing but silence.

280
00:18:41,250 --> 00:18:45,300
Look, I'm a country quack who knows a little medicine.

281
00:18:46,430 --> 00:18:49,300
When the illness is a cold,

282
00:18:49,340 --> 00:18:52,710
I can prescribe some herbal tea or the like.

283
00:18:52,950 --> 00:18:54,980
Remember the very first day?

284
00:18:55,030 --> 00:18:58,140
I prescribed a quick trip to San Francisco.

285
00:18:58,470 --> 00:19:00,160
The patient didn't follow my orders.

286
00:19:00,200 --> 00:19:04,320
Now the disease has taken root and is past all cure,

287
00:19:05,610 --> 00:19:08,590
I must take the patient to a real doctor,

288
00:19:10,190 --> 00:19:12,975
who might prescribe another city.

289
00:19:13,070 --> 00:19:15,040
That's as far as my medical knowledge goes.

290
00:19:15,080 --> 00:19:18,660
If you don't trust my prescription, go see Dr. Naser-ol-Hokama.

291
00:19:18,710 --> 00:19:19,600
Uncle Asdollah!

292
00:19:19,640 --> 00:19:21,620
You mean you're leaving me on my own?

293
00:19:21,660 --> 00:19:25,220
No, my boy, but right now there's nothing I can do.

294
00:19:25,270 --> 00:19:26,720
First we must wait and see

295
00:19:26,760 --> 00:19:30,000
what becomes of your Dear Uncle Napoleon's exile to St Helena,

296
00:19:30,040 --> 00:19:32,240
then we'll think of something.

297
00:19:33,330 --> 00:19:36,765
Now get up, stop moping. Up!

298
00:19:43,550 --> 00:19:46,140
You look pale.

299
00:19:46,490 --> 00:19:48,625
Didn't you sleep well last night?

300
00:19:50,910 --> 00:19:53,565
You'd better go and rest.

301
00:19:55,470 --> 00:19:57,760
We have done our resting.

302
00:19:59,090 --> 00:20:01,180
Now is the time to stay awake.

303
00:20:02,790 --> 00:20:11,300
Asdollah, I may have grown weak from fatigue,

304
00:20:12,270 --> 00:20:18,560
but I want them to know that a fighting man

305
00:20:19,750 --> 00:20:24,960
is a fighting man even in captivity.

306
00:20:26,810 --> 00:20:31,360
I don't want the English to see me weak.

307
00:20:31,400 --> 00:20:34,840
Doesn't a fighting man need rest too?

308
00:20:35,590 --> 00:20:39,620
Even Napoleon, while waiting for the Allied armies,

309
00:20:39,660 --> 00:20:41,310
never missed his sleep.

310
00:20:41,350 --> 00:20:42,180
Oh, yes.

311
00:20:42,230 --> 00:20:50,200
The English want to capture me broken and finished,

312
00:20:51,490 --> 00:20:57,420
so they can blacken my name in history.

313
00:21:21,710 --> 00:21:22,870
Ghasem!

314
00:21:28,110 --> 00:21:29,160
Yes, Sir?

315
00:21:29,200 --> 00:21:37,200
Did you put my gaiters and cloak in the suitcase?

316
00:21:38,630 --> 00:21:41,480
The ones you strap over your shoes?

317
00:21:41,970 --> 00:21:45,200
Yes, those. There were two pairs.

318
00:21:46,590 --> 00:21:48,200
And the cloak.

319
00:21:56,950 --> 00:21:58,300
Ghasem!

320
00:21:58,650 --> 00:22:01,300
What's going on?

321
00:22:01,600 --> 00:22:02,950
Ghasem!

322
00:22:03,150 --> 00:22:05,900
Looks like they've come.

323
00:22:17,490 --> 00:22:20,700
I've put all my affairs in order.

324
00:22:20,760 --> 00:22:24,720
Now I can go into captivity with an easy mind.

325
00:22:24,760 --> 00:22:27,760
I have just one request.

326
00:22:27,810 --> 00:22:29,150
Sir is ill!

327
00:22:29,200 --> 00:22:30,760
Asdollah!

328
00:22:30,800 --> 00:22:34,400
Go and see what that idiot Ghasem is doing.

329
00:22:34,440 --> 00:22:37,520
He seems to be resisting.

330
00:22:37,560 --> 00:22:41,400
I ordered him not to resist.

331
00:22:41,440 --> 00:22:42,560
Where's that creep?

332
00:22:42,600 --> 00:22:44,160
What's wrong? It's Mr. Dustali Khan!

333
00:22:44,200 --> 00:22:46,720
Oh! Throw that man in jail!

334
00:22:46,760 --> 00:22:48,720
Oh, blood!

335
00:22:48,760 --> 00:22:50,660
He split my head open!

336
00:22:50,700 --> 00:22:52,820
Dustali, what's wrong with you?

337
00:22:52,860 --> 00:22:54,340
Calm down.

338
00:22:54,970 --> 00:22:56,020
He split my head open!

339
00:22:56,060 --> 00:22:57,700
Murdering scoundrel!

340
00:22:57,740 --> 00:22:59,180
Oh no!

341
00:22:59,220 --> 00:23:01,780
My head! God! Blood!

342
00:23:01,820 --> 00:23:05,400
Shut up, Dustali! Can't you see Sir is unwell?

343
00:23:05,440 --> 00:23:06,720
You ungrateful wretch!

344
00:23:06,760 --> 00:23:07,760
Shut up, you disgrace!

345
00:23:07,800 --> 00:23:09,580
Shame on this family for having you as kin!

346
00:23:09,620 --> 00:23:12,000
Dustali, what the hell? What happened? Quiet!

347
00:23:12,040 --> 00:23:13,520
But the scoundrel won't let me!

348
00:23:13,560 --> 00:23:14,785
Moment!

349
00:23:15,430 --> 00:23:18,995
Whoever hit you on the head clearly hit the right spot.

350
00:23:19,690 --> 00:23:21,280
Lost what little sense you had?

351
00:23:21,320 --> 00:23:23,120
Why are you snapping at me for no reason?

352
00:23:23,160 --> 00:23:26,480
You sent Farrokh Lagha to us last night with condolences, you scoundrel!

353
00:23:26,520 --> 00:23:28,160
For my Uncle Mansur-ol-Saltaneh?

354
00:23:28,200 --> 00:23:31,340
What did my poor uncle ever do to him

355
00:23:31,380 --> 00:23:32,980
that he wishes him dead so badly?

356
00:23:33,020 --> 00:23:33,820
Why?

357
00:23:33,860 --> 00:23:36,080
Dustali, what's wrong with you?

358
00:23:36,120 --> 00:23:41,500
At a critical time like this, there's no room for quarrels.

359
00:23:41,540 --> 00:23:43,360
Tell us what happened.

360
00:23:43,400 --> 00:23:46,160
Either the whole family signs this petition,

361
00:23:46,200 --> 00:23:48,140
or I'll never speak this family's name again.

362
00:23:48,180 --> 00:23:49,640
I'm not one of you!

363
00:23:49,680 --> 00:23:51,400
What petition?

364
00:23:51,440 --> 00:23:52,920
Why is your head bandaged?

365
00:23:52,960 --> 00:23:56,800
Ask that low-life thug you married your girl off to!

366
00:23:56,840 --> 00:24:00,300
That disgraceful scoundrel of an opium addict!

367
00:24:00,340 --> 00:24:05,960
Ask that son of a bitch Ghias Abadi, who split my head with a brick!

368
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:08,180
Bravo, Aspiran Ghias Abadi!

369
00:24:08,220 --> 00:24:09,300
Well done!

370
00:24:09,340 --> 00:24:11,000
You see, Sir?

371
00:24:11,040 --> 00:24:13,760
You see? You see?

372
00:24:13,800 --> 00:24:16,920
Well, what does this petition say?

373
00:24:16,960 --> 00:24:17,920
Here.

374
00:24:17,960 --> 00:24:21,600
This is Dr. Naser-ol-Hokama's certificate, confirming

375
00:24:21,640 --> 00:24:23,840
that Qamar is mentally ill.

376
00:24:23,880 --> 00:24:26,600
And this is a sworn statement from the neighbors.

377
00:24:26,640 --> 00:24:32,440
Sir, this poor girl is insane!

378
00:24:32,480 --> 00:24:34,720
That charlatan Ghias Abadi

379
00:24:34,760 --> 00:24:37,480
is eating up her entire fortune!

380
00:24:37,520 --> 00:24:40,760
He's selling off the Akbarabad lands!

381
00:24:43,520 --> 00:24:46,200
"To the honorable gentlemen who know

382
00:24:46,240 --> 00:24:49,000
that the Mahmudabad estate belonged to the undersigned..."

383
00:24:49,040 --> 00:24:50,090
Shut up, Dustali!

384
00:24:50,130 --> 00:24:50,930
They're here!

385
00:24:50,970 --> 00:24:52,700
Sir is ill, you can't!

386
00:24:53,480 --> 00:24:55,550
Where's that Dustali?

387
00:24:56,240 --> 00:24:59,480
I'll give you a hiding they'll tell stories about!

388
00:24:59,520 --> 00:25:00,480
Mr. Aspiran!

389
00:25:00,520 --> 00:25:02,480
Show your son-in-law some respect!

390
00:25:02,520 --> 00:25:04,480
To hell with this son-in-law of mine!

391
00:25:04,520 --> 00:25:05,960
How dare you!

392
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:10,960
Never mind you! Your father and grandfather should be

393
00:25:11,000 --> 00:25:11,960
proud of a son-in-law like him!

394
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:14,960
Not in a hundred years will a crook like that be my son-in-law!

395
00:25:15,000 --> 00:25:17,960
I'll smash your face in!

396
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:20,000
Out of the way!

397
00:25:21,960 --> 00:25:26,760
He's taken advantage of the girl!

398
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:32,290
Excuse me.

399
00:25:37,840 --> 00:25:39,650
You picked a fine time, too.

400
00:25:40,200 --> 00:25:40,850
Get out.

401
00:25:40,900 --> 00:25:42,905
Look, this matter...

402
00:25:44,500 --> 00:25:48,100
God, forget the English, save me from this...

403
00:25:52,640 --> 00:25:54,190
Get out!

404
00:25:58,200 --> 00:26:00,335
I'll give you your money back.

405
00:26:13,220 --> 00:26:14,860
He hit me, he hit me!

406
00:26:16,640 --> 00:26:18,710
You've broken my bones!

407
00:26:19,500 --> 00:26:22,025
Put me down! Put me down!

408
00:26:23,740 --> 00:26:31,740
Then what are the English waiting for?

409
00:26:36,200 --> 00:26:37,000
What?

410
00:26:37,000 --> 00:26:37,960
Has Sir been taken ill?

411
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:38,960
Oh, good Lord!

412
00:26:39,000 --> 00:26:39,960
Sir!

413
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:41,960
God, see us through this!

414
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:42,960
Sir!

415
00:26:43,000 --> 00:26:43,960
Sir!

416
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:44,960
It's his heart!

417
00:26:45,000 --> 00:26:45,960
Calm down, Mash Ghasem.

418
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:47,095
Sir!

419
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:49,960
Mash Ghasem, go fetch the doctor.

420
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:52,760
Right away, there's a good boy!

421
00:26:52,800 --> 00:26:53,600
What is it?

422
00:26:53,640 --> 00:26:54,950
He'll be fine in a moment.

423
00:26:55,000 --> 00:26:56,050
Quick, lift him up.

424
00:26:56,100 --> 00:26:57,630
He'll be fine in a moment.

425
00:26:57,680 --> 00:27:01,380
I hope that oaf Naser-ol-Hokama is at his clinic.

426
00:27:01,420 --> 00:27:03,460
His pulse is very weak.

427
00:27:03,500 --> 00:27:05,340
You must...

428
00:27:06,020 --> 00:27:07,540
You must...

429
00:27:07,580 --> 00:27:10,510
come with me.

430
00:27:10,560 --> 00:27:14,160
I have so much to say.

431
00:27:16,720 --> 00:27:20,040
They'll come, for sure.

432
00:27:21,000 --> 00:27:23,980
They'll be here any moment.

433
00:27:25,140 --> 00:27:30,220
God, give me the strength

434
00:27:30,900 --> 00:27:34,300
to stand on my own two feet

435
00:27:35,320 --> 00:27:40,000
and face them.

436
00:27:40,240 --> 00:27:42,740
They've come!

437
00:27:43,780 --> 00:27:45,340
They've come!

438
00:28:15,980 --> 00:28:19,675
I am representative of whole army of British Empire.

439
00:28:23,660 --> 00:28:26,500
Mister Churchill send you his special regards.

440
00:28:26,540 --> 00:28:29,780
Mister Churchill order you must surrender.

441
00:28:29,820 --> 00:28:32,410
Excuse me, you must forgive me,

442
00:28:32,860 --> 00:28:35,360
but I am officer, must arrest you.

443
00:28:35,400 --> 00:28:38,185
Please, you make no resistance.

444
00:28:39,380 --> 00:28:40,960
I ordered

445
00:28:42,220 --> 00:28:45,160
Ghasem

446
00:28:45,200 --> 00:28:48,060
not to resist.

447
00:28:48,100 --> 00:28:49,680
General...

448
00:28:50,580 --> 00:28:52,820
Great General...

449
00:28:53,480 --> 00:28:56,460
I am at your disposal.

450
00:28:57,460 --> 00:28:58,750
Sit down.

451
00:29:02,480 --> 00:29:03,980
His pulse has become very weak.

452
00:29:04,020 --> 00:29:05,765
It's irregular.

453
00:29:06,160 --> 00:29:08,100
I hope Naser-ol-Hokama gets here soon.

454
00:29:08,140 --> 00:29:11,575
I'll go and phone Dr. Sedtai Khan too.

455
00:29:24,460 --> 00:29:26,010
Excuse me.

456
00:29:27,000 --> 00:29:29,000
Baron, Baron Ardavaz!

457
00:29:29,050 --> 00:29:30,795
Wait, wait.

458
00:29:31,660 --> 00:29:32,460
Take it.

459
00:29:32,500 --> 00:29:33,770
No, no, on your life, I can't.

460
00:29:33,820 --> 00:29:34,910
I'm in your debt.

461
00:29:34,960 --> 00:29:37,290
At least take this for the flag cloth, Baron dear.

462
00:29:37,340 --> 00:29:40,010
My friend, every house has these flags.

463
00:29:40,060 --> 00:29:40,670
Oh, come on.

464
00:29:40,720 --> 00:29:42,110
On my life, Ardavaz.

465
00:29:42,160 --> 00:29:44,880
My friend, you asked us one small favor,

466
00:29:44,920 --> 00:29:46,020
and we take money for it?

467
00:29:46,060 --> 00:29:47,700
I'm begging you, on my life!

468
00:29:47,740 --> 00:29:48,840
We didn't do anything.

469
00:29:48,880 --> 00:29:50,740
We put on this soldier's uniform in the garden,

470
00:29:50,780 --> 00:29:52,100
came in and said a few words.

471
00:29:52,140 --> 00:29:52,940
That's...

472
00:29:52,980 --> 00:29:54,405
Now I'm getting angry!

473
00:29:54,880 --> 00:29:56,460
Well, since you insist, all right.

474
00:29:56,500 --> 00:29:58,220
But honestly, it embarrasses us.

475
00:29:58,260 --> 00:29:59,240
Insist? Nonsense.

476
00:29:59,280 --> 00:30:00,260
Thank you kindly.

477
00:30:00,300 --> 00:30:01,990
Yes, yes, this way.

478
00:30:02,040 --> 00:30:03,330
Thanks.

479
00:30:05,200 --> 00:30:05,830
Seriously.

480
00:30:05,880 --> 00:30:07,600
Much obliged. Bravo. Superb.

481
00:30:07,640 --> 00:30:08,750
But keep it between us.

482
00:30:08,800 --> 00:30:10,250
Quick, change, change your clothes.

483
00:30:10,300 --> 00:30:11,570
I'll come see you tonight.

484
00:30:11,620 --> 00:30:14,405
Thanks a lot. Much obliged. Bravo.

485
00:30:14,680 --> 00:30:16,640
I hope I can repay you someday.

486
00:30:16,680 --> 00:30:17,710
At your service, Highness.

487
00:30:17,750 --> 00:30:18,660
And at yours.

488
00:30:18,700 --> 00:30:19,630
See you soon.

489
00:30:19,680 --> 00:30:20,640
Thanks. See you soon.

490
00:30:20,680 --> 00:30:22,880
Say hi to the boys.

491
00:30:25,680 --> 00:30:27,680
Bravo, Shazdeh!

492
00:30:30,040 --> 00:30:32,430
Well done! Where did you find him?

493
00:30:32,480 --> 00:30:33,870
That's Ardavaz, of course!

494
00:30:33,920 --> 00:30:35,590
Baron Ardavaz, the liquor seller.

495
00:30:35,640 --> 00:30:37,690
His bar is where the boys hang out.

496
00:30:37,740 --> 00:30:39,490
He looked just like an Englishman!

497
00:30:39,540 --> 00:30:41,030
I really thought he was English.

498
00:30:41,080 --> 00:30:43,995
The boys call him Mister Ardavaz.

499
00:30:44,420 --> 00:30:46,945
I'd had my eye on him for days.

500
00:30:47,380 --> 00:30:48,680
How's Sir doing?

501
00:30:48,720 --> 00:30:49,815
Huh?

502
00:31:16,410 --> 00:31:19,050
In my opinion, his condition is very grave.

503
00:31:19,090 --> 00:31:23,900
Let Dr. Naser-ol-Hokama examine him and give his final verdict.

504
00:31:23,950 --> 00:31:26,690
Well, of course, his opinion is sound too.

505
00:31:35,650 --> 00:31:40,600
May God and Saint Zahra crush them! All Sir suffers is their doing!

506
00:31:40,650 --> 00:31:41,400
Mash Ghasem!

507
00:31:41,450 --> 00:31:46,430
Honestly, we'd stake our beard on it: they've poisoned Sir.

508
00:31:46,470 --> 00:31:48,060
What? Poisoned him?

509
00:31:48,110 --> 00:31:50,770
Why should I lie, Doctor? The grave is this close!

510
00:31:50,810 --> 00:31:51,640
Come on, Mash Ghasem!

511
00:31:51,680 --> 00:31:53,300
Stop this nonsense!

512
00:31:53,340 --> 00:31:55,345
Let him speak.

513
00:31:55,530 --> 00:31:58,940
As it happens, I too have noticed signs of poisoning.

514
00:31:58,980 --> 00:32:03,800
My dear doctor, I've treated this patient for years.

515
00:32:03,840 --> 00:32:08,760
He's had these symptoms in every heart attack before.

516
00:32:08,800 --> 00:32:11,420
Yes, I know, Doctor, you're right.

517
00:32:11,460 --> 00:32:14,120
You may know the patient better than I do,

518
00:32:14,160 --> 00:32:16,340
but I am a forensic doctor.

519
00:32:16,380 --> 00:32:19,540
From morning to night I see a hundred poisoned patients.

520
00:32:19,580 --> 00:32:31,500
Doctor, see a thousand! These symptoms are normal in complete heart block.

521
00:32:31,540 --> 00:32:34,800
Please don't teach me medicine.

522
00:32:34,840 --> 00:32:39,420
If I see poisoning, my duty is to report it to the proper authorities.

523
00:32:39,460 --> 00:32:44,580
Why are you obstructing my legal inquiry, sir?

524
00:32:45,380 --> 00:32:48,880
Then what are you waiting for? Go ahead, report it!

525
00:32:48,920 --> 00:32:53,410
Mr. Forensic Doctor, you only thought of poison when this peasant spoke up.

526
00:32:53,450 --> 00:32:54,250
Yes.

527
00:32:54,290 --> 00:32:56,000
Why didn't you see signs of poisoning before?

528
00:32:56,040 --> 00:32:57,200
Bless you!

529
00:32:57,240 --> 00:32:59,760
Moment, gentlemen!

530
00:32:59,800 --> 00:33:02,930
Please hear the rest of what Mash Ghasem has to say.

531
00:33:02,980 --> 00:33:06,410
Mash Ghasem, who do you think poisoned Sir?

532
00:33:06,460 --> 00:33:08,140
Well, honestly, Sir, why should I lie?

533
00:33:08,180 --> 00:33:09,960
The grave is this close!

534
00:33:10,000 --> 00:33:12,830
We didn't see anything with our own eyes,

535
00:33:12,870 --> 00:33:17,120
but we're sure it was the English who poisoned Sir.

536
00:33:17,170 --> 00:33:18,580
Did you hear that?

537
00:33:18,630 --> 00:33:22,760
Mash Ghasem believes the British Empire poisoned Sir.

538
00:33:22,810 --> 00:33:25,920
Who? The British Empire?

539
00:33:26,570 --> 00:33:29,800
That's something the autopsy must establish.

540
00:33:29,850 --> 00:33:32,880
First they must remove the opium from the patient's stomach,

541
00:33:32,920 --> 00:33:35,620
then have it analyzed at the forensic lab,

542
00:33:35,670 --> 00:33:39,880
and if the opium turns out to be made in England,

543
00:33:39,920 --> 00:33:43,340
send a bailiff after Mr. Churchill at once!

544
00:33:43,380 --> 00:33:44,340
Bless you!

545
00:33:44,380 --> 00:33:47,340
One should be suspicious of every death.

546
00:33:47,380 --> 00:33:49,340
Why are you making fun, sir?

547
00:33:49,380 --> 00:33:50,340
I'm quite serious, sir.

548
00:33:50,380 --> 00:33:53,340
Moment, moment, gentlemen! Your human and professional duty

549
00:33:53,380 --> 00:33:54,840
is to think of the patient,

550
00:33:54,880 --> 00:33:56,340
not to squabble over trifles.

551
00:33:56,380 --> 00:33:57,180
Yes.

552
00:33:57,220 --> 00:33:59,320
Please, carry on this debate in the other room.

553
00:33:59,370 --> 00:34:01,700
Please, go ahead.

554
00:34:02,510 --> 00:34:03,940
After you.

555
00:34:03,990 --> 00:34:05,850
No, after you. Please.

556
00:34:05,900 --> 00:34:07,515
Bless you.

557
00:34:10,070 --> 00:34:13,500
Mash Ghasem, run and get a car. We'll take Sir to the hospital.

558
00:34:13,550 --> 00:34:14,645
Water.

559
00:34:16,250 --> 00:34:18,900
Water. I want water, sir.

560
00:34:37,350 --> 00:34:38,870
Sir is gone.

561
00:34:43,250 --> 00:34:45,840
Sir didn't take us with him.

562
00:35:46,160 --> 00:35:52,940
Napoleon was born in 1769, and Dear Uncle in some unknown year.

563
00:35:53,750 --> 00:35:57,580
Napoleon died in 1821,

564
00:35:57,620 --> 00:36:01,200
and Dear Uncle in 1941.

565
00:36:09,430 --> 00:36:11,270
Hasn't he come to yet?

566
00:36:11,320 --> 00:36:15,320
Send someone by car to Ab-Ali to fetch the Colonel.

567
00:36:16,370 --> 00:36:19,155
Have them break it to him gently so he won't worry.

568
00:36:21,670 --> 00:36:24,650
And he mustn't bring Leili with him.

569
00:36:29,930 --> 00:36:31,285
What's happened?

570
00:36:41,410 --> 00:36:44,000
We must get to the root of this.

571
00:36:44,050 --> 00:36:49,190
As I said, I'm a forensic doctor; naturally I see this differently.

572
00:36:49,230 --> 00:36:50,580
Bless you.

573
00:36:50,620 --> 00:36:53,180
That's no reason why my diagnosis,

574
00:36:53,230 --> 00:36:54,580
who's watched this patient for years,

575
00:36:54,620 --> 00:36:56,580
should bow to yours...

576
00:36:56,620 --> 00:36:58,580
First of all, you...

577
00:36:58,620 --> 00:37:00,650
First of all, you never diagnosed poisoning!

578
00:37:00,700 --> 00:37:03,400
Gentlemen, I agree.

579
00:37:03,481 --> 00:37:07,381
We must see whether his heart is sound,

580
00:37:07,600 --> 00:37:09,382
still working well,

581
00:37:09,422 --> 00:37:10,222
or not.

582
00:37:10,262 --> 00:37:12,052
Of course it is, my dear fellow.

583
00:37:12,102 --> 00:37:14,452
It works like clockwork.

584
00:37:14,502 --> 00:37:16,442
Why wouldn't you be sure?

585
00:37:19,682 --> 00:37:21,492
Saeid, let's go.

586
00:37:22,082 --> 00:37:24,352
I want to stay here, Uncle Asdollah.

587
00:37:24,402 --> 00:37:26,017
Stay and do what?

588
00:37:26,302 --> 00:37:27,492
Are you an oxygen specialist?

589
00:37:27,542 --> 00:37:28,767
Come on.

590
00:37:52,022 --> 00:37:54,222
That owl's turned up again.

591
00:38:55,982 --> 00:38:57,662
Uncle Asdollah.

592
00:38:58,380 --> 00:39:02,600
You told Agha Jan to send for Uncle Colonel at Ab-Ali, didn't you?

593
00:39:04,000 --> 00:39:06,280
Well, yes. Yes.

594
00:39:06,940 --> 00:39:10,380
Then why did you say Uncle Colonel shouldn't bring Leili?

595
00:39:10,420 --> 00:39:12,292
Were you thinking of me?

596
00:39:12,342 --> 00:39:13,957
Why of you?

597
00:39:14,620 --> 00:39:19,580
If Dear Uncle came to, he might have Seyed Abolghasem marry Leili to Pouri.

598
00:39:19,620 --> 00:39:23,120
You didn't want Leili to marry Pouri, did you?

599
00:39:23,620 --> 00:39:25,820
Last night, while you were ill,

600
00:39:26,620 --> 00:39:29,145
before Leili left for Ab-Ali,

601
00:39:29,620 --> 00:39:33,510
your Dear Uncle sent Seyed Abolghasem for the notary.

602
00:39:34,620 --> 00:39:39,580
If Dear Uncle sent for the notary, why didn't they marry Leili to Pouri?

603
00:39:39,620 --> 00:39:41,580
They did marry them.

604
00:39:41,620 --> 00:39:43,620
It's done.

605
00:41:15,342 --> 00:41:16,437
No.

606
00:41:16,701 --> 00:41:18,482
No, my foot!

607
00:41:18,842 --> 00:41:20,327
Come on, drink.

608
00:41:36,022 --> 00:41:37,247
Drink.

609
00:41:51,082 --> 00:41:53,672
I was once like you,

610
00:41:55,842 --> 00:41:58,060
sensitive, easily hurt.

611
00:42:00,042 --> 00:42:02,177
But life changed me.

612
00:42:04,680 --> 00:42:08,050
Our bodies are made in our mother's workshop,

613
00:42:09,222 --> 00:42:11,942
but our souls in the workshop of the world.

614
00:42:17,762 --> 00:42:20,822
Have you heard the story of my marriage?

615
00:42:20,920 --> 00:42:22,320
I know a little.

616
00:42:22,360 --> 00:42:25,540
I mean, I heard you married and then divorced her.

617
00:42:25,580 --> 00:42:27,390
As simple as that?

618
00:42:28,082 --> 00:42:30,672
I married her and then divorced her?

619
00:42:32,042 --> 00:42:33,560
Drink up and I'll tell you.

620
00:42:33,600 --> 00:42:35,605
No more, I won't drink.

621
00:42:36,622 --> 00:42:39,212
Moment! Like hell you won't!

622
00:42:42,820 --> 00:42:44,820
Cheers.

623
00:42:53,860 --> 00:42:56,860
I was seventeen or eighteen when I fell in love.

624
00:43:03,180 --> 00:43:05,180
With whom?

625
00:43:05,220 --> 00:43:07,225
A distant relative.

626
00:43:08,020 --> 00:43:11,180
A cousin of our black-clad Farrokh Lagha.

627
00:43:11,220 --> 00:43:13,180
Was she pretty?

628
00:43:13,220 --> 00:43:14,180
Very.

629
00:43:14,220 --> 00:43:16,180
Did she love you too?

630
00:43:16,220 --> 00:43:18,220
Very much.

631
00:43:20,220 --> 00:43:24,220
You have no say in childhood and youthful love.

632
00:43:29,220 --> 00:43:33,220
Parents make their kids fall in love.

633
00:43:34,150 --> 00:43:37,280
One keeps jokingly calling the other's daughter "my bride",

634
00:43:37,330 --> 00:43:40,115
the other calls his son "my son-in-law",

635
00:43:40,430 --> 00:43:43,440
and they keep drumming it into your ears.

636
00:43:44,850 --> 00:43:47,020
Then, when you're old enough to fall in love,

637
00:43:47,060 --> 00:43:50,365
you find you've fallen for the very bride your parents picked.

638
00:43:51,450 --> 00:43:54,625
But those same parents, once they found out,

639
00:43:55,590 --> 00:43:58,635
made life hell for me and that girl.

640
00:44:02,130 --> 00:44:03,140
But why?

641
00:44:03,190 --> 00:44:07,730
Her father had found her a richer husband than me.

642
00:44:08,110 --> 00:44:12,410
My father had found his son a bride with a grander name.

643
00:44:13,570 --> 00:44:15,740
But we didn't back down.

644
00:44:15,790 --> 00:44:18,640
We took so many beatings and insults

645
00:44:18,930 --> 00:44:22,820
that they gave up and had to let us marry.

646
00:44:31,730 --> 00:44:35,730
For two whole years I never even thought of another woman.

647
00:44:36,510 --> 00:44:39,860
This world and the next, sleep and waking, past and future,

648
00:44:39,900 --> 00:44:43,400
everything there was, for me, was in that woman.

649
00:44:52,250 --> 00:44:53,500
For a year or so, she too

650
00:44:53,540 --> 00:44:56,650
seemed to feel the same way about me.

651
00:44:57,270 --> 00:45:00,250
But little by little I changed in her eyes.

652
00:45:05,350 --> 00:45:07,260
But you loved each other so much.

653
00:45:07,300 --> 00:45:09,080
Why did it turn out like this?

654
00:45:09,120 --> 00:45:11,740
I had a friend who always said,

655
00:45:11,780 --> 00:45:13,560
"The first year I was married,

656
00:45:13,600 --> 00:45:15,780
my wife was so lovely and sweet

657
00:45:15,820 --> 00:45:18,150
I could have eaten her up.

658
00:45:18,550 --> 00:45:19,640
But by the third year,

659
00:45:19,680 --> 00:45:23,050
I was sorry I hadn't eaten her up that first year."

660
00:45:31,900 --> 00:45:36,400
I can't be bothered telling you why or how it happened.

661
00:45:38,310 --> 00:45:42,010
I'll just tell you that from the second year on, if

662
00:45:44,800 --> 00:45:49,750
I came straight home from the office and went nowhere else,

663
00:45:49,800 --> 00:45:52,820
my wife thought I had nowhere to go.

664
00:45:52,870 --> 00:45:55,300
If I didn't look at other women,

665
00:45:55,350 --> 00:45:57,615
she thought I didn't have it in me.

666
00:45:59,230 --> 00:46:02,730
Remember how often you asked me whose picture this is?

667
00:46:03,930 --> 00:46:05,675
This friend of yours?

668
00:46:05,730 --> 00:46:07,140
Yes, I remember.

669
00:46:07,190 --> 00:46:10,200
Moment, moment.

670
00:46:10,290 --> 00:46:13,660
I always told you he was an old friend of mine.

671
00:46:13,810 --> 00:46:15,920
But he wasn't my friend.

672
00:46:15,970 --> 00:46:18,750
He was my savior.

673
00:46:18,850 --> 00:46:20,870
Your savior?

674
00:46:21,720 --> 00:46:22,815
Yes.

675
00:46:24,520 --> 00:46:26,880
My savior.

676
00:46:28,040 --> 00:46:30,640
Bless his ugly heart!

677
00:46:31,180 --> 00:46:35,380
My wife ran off with this uncouth, hulking Arab.

678
00:46:37,380 --> 00:46:38,880
Ran off?

679
00:46:39,420 --> 00:46:40,910
And you did nothing?

680
00:46:40,960 --> 00:46:42,510
I divorced her.

681
00:46:44,840 --> 00:46:46,260
She went and married

682
00:46:46,380 --> 00:46:48,600
this Abdolghader Baghdadi.

683
00:46:48,640 --> 00:46:53,040
This guy stole your wife, and you framed his photo to look at?

684
00:46:57,000 --> 00:46:59,395
You're still a child. Come, sit down.

685
00:46:59,760 --> 00:47:00,855
Come.

686
00:47:03,640 --> 00:47:06,240
If you were drowning at sea,

687
00:47:06,640 --> 00:47:11,240
and just as your life was slipping away, a whale appeared

688
00:47:11,600 --> 00:47:13,085
and saved you,

689
00:47:14,040 --> 00:47:16,000
it would look to you

690
00:47:16,040 --> 00:47:19,220
more beautiful than any film star.

691
00:47:21,490 --> 00:47:23,820
Hideous Abdolghader

692
00:47:23,860 --> 00:47:26,860
is that whale, who to me

693
00:47:26,900 --> 00:47:29,685
is more beautiful than Marlene Dietrich.

694
00:47:31,130 --> 00:47:33,135
Give it here, let me get a good look.

695
00:47:35,210 --> 00:47:37,865
So why did she fall for Abdolghader?

696
00:47:38,560 --> 00:47:41,345
I spoke to my wife with refinement,

697
00:47:42,210 --> 00:47:44,800
Abdolghader with crudeness and force.

698
00:47:45,170 --> 00:47:47,825
I bathed once a day,

699
00:47:48,390 --> 00:47:50,655
Abdolghader once a month.

700
00:47:50,830 --> 00:47:52,760
I wouldn't even eat a radish with my lunch,

701
00:47:52,800 --> 00:47:56,800
Abdolghader ate garlic and radishes by the kilo.

702
00:47:58,070 --> 00:47:59,580
I recited Saadi's poetry,

703
00:47:59,620 --> 00:48:01,820
Abdolghader belched.

704
00:48:10,750 --> 00:48:12,380
And yet, in my wife's eyes,

705
00:48:12,420 --> 00:48:15,780
I was the dull one, Abdolghader the clever one.

706
00:48:15,820 --> 00:48:18,920
I was the coarse one, Abdolghader the refined one.

707
00:48:24,230 --> 00:48:25,910
Only, I suspect,

708
00:48:26,570 --> 00:48:29,420
Abdolghader was a good traveler.

709
00:48:30,270 --> 00:48:32,470
When it came to travel, he was a marvel.

710
00:48:32,870 --> 00:48:36,175
One foot here, one foot in San Francisco.

711
00:48:40,130 --> 00:48:41,745
Uncle Asdollah,

712
00:48:42,210 --> 00:48:44,440
why did you tell me all this?

713
00:48:44,480 --> 00:48:46,960
Your eyes need opening a little.

714
00:48:47,000 --> 00:48:50,630
Things you'll learn later, whether you like it or not,

715
00:48:51,370 --> 00:48:53,140
I want you to understand sooner.

716
00:48:53,180 --> 00:48:54,840
You mean Leili too, like...

717
00:48:54,880 --> 00:48:56,140
No, no, no.

718
00:48:56,180 --> 00:48:58,250
I meant nothing of the sort.

719
00:49:00,210 --> 00:49:01,360
I only meant that

720
00:49:01,400 --> 00:49:03,460
if Leili goes off with Pouri,

721
00:49:03,500 --> 00:49:05,700
you haven't lost anything important.

722
00:49:07,786 --> 00:49:12,406
If one day, for some Abdolghader from Baghdad or Mosul,

723
00:49:14,310 --> 00:49:20,270
she's going to leave you, better she goes with Pouri now.

724
00:49:21,106 --> 00:49:22,201
No.

725
00:49:23,266 --> 00:49:27,806
But Uncle Asdollah, this Pouri is nothing like Abdolghader.

726
00:49:27,950 --> 00:49:31,290
Don't compare anyone's love with mine.

727
00:49:31,340 --> 00:49:33,460
You don't know how much I love Leili.

728
00:49:33,500 --> 00:49:35,560
You've been in love yourself,

729
00:49:35,600 --> 00:49:36,740
but my love...

730
00:49:36,780 --> 00:49:39,020
Your love is greater than all other loves.

731
00:49:39,060 --> 00:49:41,260
I have no doubt.

732
00:49:41,300 --> 00:49:43,825
Because it's your first love.

733
00:49:47,166 --> 00:49:49,520
But let me tell you something.

734
00:49:50,706 --> 00:49:53,166
Leili isn't what matters most here.

735
00:49:54,806 --> 00:49:57,851
What matters is that you've come to know love.

736
00:49:58,526 --> 00:50:00,500
That's the threshold of manhood.

737
00:50:12,006 --> 00:50:19,600
On a hot summer day, the 13th of Mordad, at a quarter to three, I fell in love.

